      whyItWorks:
        "Why is a parabola symmetric? Write {{y = x^2 - 2x - 3}} as {{y = (x - 1)^2 - 4}}. Now move the same distance d either side of x = 1: at x = 1 + d and x = 1 − d, {{(x - 1)^2}} is {{d^2}} both times, so y is the same. The only thing that matters is the *distance* from x = 1 — that's exactly what a mirror line means. And since {{(x - 1)^2 >= 0}}, the smallest y is −4, when the bracket is zero.",
